part  in shaping Indian civilization and in  determining the customary roles of women  Nehru was mostly tone-deaf his discovery  or maybe it's an invention of a  civilization wonderfully attractive to  secular intellectuals bear some  resemblance to the Zionist discovery or  invention of biblical Israel neither one  came to grips with the actual religious  beliefs and practices of the people who  had to be liberated maybe that wasn't  possible maybe it was necessary first to  lay down the secular law as Nehru and  Ambedkar did in the new Indian  Constitution and Civil Code and only  when these turn out to be incompletely  effective and when they provoke a  religious reaction do the engagements  and negotiations have to begin so this  is the question that secular liberals  and Democrats must face  how can National Liberation make itself  central to the ongoing life of the  nation how can it regain and sustain its  political hegemony and this is this is  the the deepest question that I mean to  raise in these lectures and which the  Indian writers I've just  and quoting have begun to address what  has gone wrong with and what are the  obstacles to the cultural reproduction  of let's call it the secular left  liberation cannot succeed hegemony  cannot be sustained by negation alone  that's the beginning of an answer to  these questions the fact of hegemony  rights Antonio Gramsci its foremost  theorist presupposes that one takes into  account the interests and tendencies of  the groups over which hegemony will be  exercised and it also presupposes a  certain equilibrium that is to say that  the hegemonic groups will make some  sacrifices fierce and unrelenting  hostility may be crucial to the  liberation movements initial initial  mobilization and it necessarily figures  also in secular responses to the  traditionalist counter-revolution but it  isn't a creative force it doesn't take  the interests of traditional Hindus  Muslims and Jews into account and it  doesn't move toward the required  equilibrium so consider now the Zionist  case I'm going to conclude here since  this is the case with which I am myself  most engaged in Israel it seems clear  that the negation of exilic Judaism has  failed and that's the leap to the  biblical past and other efforts to find  usable bits and pieces of the tradition  serve only to produce a Jewish Kitsch  which cannot compete with a revived  Judaism the claim to a radical newness  gives rise inexorably to a radicalized  old Necedah ism as with Hinduism and  Islam it is possible that the old old  miss was in fact more pluralistic more  accepting of difference  in practice if not in doctrine then  either the secular liberationists  imagined or the religious zealots admit  maybe but I don't think that nostalgia  is any more attractive in Israel than in  India there is indeed much that needed  to be and still needs to be negated the  fearfulness and passivity of traditional  Judaism the dominance of the rabbi's the  subordination of women but alongside the  ongoing work of negation the tradition  has to be acknowledged and it's  different parts in gathered as the poet  Bialik argued collected translated  incorporated into the culture of the new  only then can traditional Judaism be  pulled apart if that's what people want  to do historical its most important  features laws and Maxim ceremonies and  practices historical and fictional  narratives critically appraised and only  then can those features be accepted or  rejected or revised only then can they  become the subject of an ongoing  argument and negotiation it is argument  itself with its varying outcomes that  constitutes the Grampian equilibrium  not any single or final balance of  acceptance and rejection one can be a  very radical critic of the religious  tradition as I would be while still  taking account of its value for the  Jewish people or for the Indian or the  Algerian people but the commitment to  take account means that one must learn  something about the world of one's  traditionalists opponents the opposite  of negation is not acceptance but again  intellectual and political engagement  since Zionism is a political movement  the most obvious area of engagement is  politics itself but Zionists negation  was first of all a denial that there was  a Jewish politics in the Exile or even a  collective Jew  history here's ben-gurion since our last  national tragedy he's referring to the  suppression of the Bar Kokhba rebellion  in the year 135 of the Common Era since  our last national tragedy we have had  histories of persecution of legal  discrimination of the Inquisition and  pogroms of martyrdom but we have not had  a Jewish history because of the history  of a people is only what the people  create as a whole and the Jews according  to ben-gurion created nothing in the  centuries of Exile in fact the internal  politics of the Jewish Diaspora through  which the people as a whole sustained  itself in scattered communities without  course of power for many centuries is  one of the more remarkable stories in  the political history of humankind  exilic politics was in its decadence  when the Zionists arrived on the scene  and had never been valued within the  tradition itself committed to deference  and deferment dreaming of a distant  triumph the rabbi's have little to say  about the success of the semi-autonomous  communities of the Diaspora and they  took no interest in the political  lessons that might be drawn from it this  could have provided an opening for  Zionists writers but they found the  decadence of Jewish communal life a more  useful subject and made no effort to  overcome they're ignorant of its better  days for some of them indeed ignorance  of exilic history since it wasn't a real  national history was a matter of  principle I've got how I'm at least  acknowledged the better days the  rabbinic sages he wrote succeeded in  creating a national body which hung in  midair without any foundation on solid  earth and in this body the Hebrew  national spirit has had its abode and  lived its life for 2,000 years  but the real achievement of exilic  jewelry marvelous and unique as Kadam  said was to lose the singularity of this  body and still survive in scattered  fragments all living one form of life  and all United  despite their local separateness these  fine words were apparently not  inspirational I have not found in the  literature of Zionism any discovery of  the old Jewish communities I mean any  extended effort to understand how exilic  politics worked or to honor the people  who made it work or to value the  achievement the laws customs practices  and implicit understandings that made  the communal life of the Exile possible  surely this is material for in gathering  and then for appreciation and critique  by a movement aiming at a new birth of  communal life indeed the experience of  exile and then of emancipation in exile  might well teach contemporary Israelis  something of great importance how  different Jewish communities could  coexist within the framework of a  secular state alongside other non-jewish  religious communities the new Israeli  majority might learn a lot from the  experience of the old Jewish minorities  another obvious place for engagement as  in India and Algeria is with the  traditional subordination of women which  took different forms in Judaism than it  did in Hinduism and in Islam never  reaching for example to the radical  seclusion of the perda  but still mandating the exclusion of  women from public religious and  political life the liberationists  commitment to create a new man was also  a commitment to create a new woman set  free from her traditionally required  submission to patriarchal Authority the  actual standing of women in the kibbutz  movement in the Hagana and then in the  Israeli army  didn't and doesn't always accord with  liberationists ideology but the  ideological position is clear in the new  nation-state there would be gender  equality I've already described how  religious revival challenges the very  idea of equality and have surprised that  unprepared the liberationists were for  the willingness of many women to return  to orthodoxy and accept a version of the  old subordination they are almost  equally surprised by the fierceness of  the religious zealots attack on gender  equality even in the Israeli army which  has been one of the prime instruments of  liberationists transformation and  something of a sacred cow to mix my  cases in Israeli politics the Galit  Aryan argument seems obvious to me but  it's also obvious that simply reciting  the argument isn't sufficient today  there are religious women in every  denomination of Judaism arguing for  equality in the language of the  tradition and producing wonderfully  subtle and intellectually engaging  reinterpretations of both the Bible and  the Talmud it may be a sign of the  polarization produced by secularists and  zealots in Israel that a great deal of  the early feminists interpretive work  was not done there but in the Diaspora  indeed much of the religious energy  among American Jews today comes from  women who make up a growing number of  ordained rabbis needless to say nothing  similar is going on in Israel where  secular Jewish women aren't interested  in becoming rabbis and religious women  wouldn't dream of it though some I think  are beginning to dream perhaps there is  a pattern here  the central office of women living under  Muslim laws is not an Algeria or an any  Muslim majority country but it is in  London and the Marquis and I'll Akhil  with Romney and uma Narayan eloquent  critics of Archimedean secularism in  India live in the United States the men  and women who reclaim tradition while  also criticizing it they are some  resemblance to the early leaders of  national liberation who also live for  long periods of time outside the  countries they meant to liberate but  arguments for a critical engagement with  the tradition will make their way home  at least they have some chance of  appealing to men and women who tire of  the radical polarity of negation and  revival in all three of my cases we can  begin to see writers and activists  struggle struggling to occupy the space  between the polls in Israel and I  suspect in India and Algeria to the  clearest examples come from the academic  world the project of critical engagement  has not yet found what is most necessary  to its success a strong political  expression I haven't meant to argue in  these lectures that the traditionalist  counter-revolution could have been  avoided the paradox of national  liberation overcome had the Zionists a  fully engaged the tradition from the  beginning but a full-scale engagement  early on might have made for a stronger  response to the counter-revolution than  anything forthcoming today it might have  provided Zionism with a more elaborated  a more interesting and a more democratic  culture and it might have improved the  odds it might still improve the odds for  the eventual success of Jewish national  liberation this suggests of you that has  become more and more appealing to me as  I have worked on these three cases  national liberation like every other  form of liberation is a very long  process not a single battle but a series  of battles that extends a  for many decades despite the engagement  of religious zealots this process is  secular in two senses of the word as the  dictionary defines the word liberation  is a worldly process and it is of  indefinite duration its secular in  another sense to its outcome is not  determined by faith not by religious  faith and not by the ideological version  either the early liberationists believed  that their struggle had a singular and  certain end but the critical engagement  with religious beliefs and practices is  liberating in a new way now the end is  open radically uncertain or better the  process is unendingly various the tasks  of men and women committed to national  liberation is to sustain their  engagement with the traditions of their  nation and at the same time their fierce  opposition to all the versions of  traditionalists passivity and oppression  now I've always disliked lectures and  writers who end by telling other people  what their task is but since the task  that I've just described has also been  my own task in these lectures I don't  mind recommending it to any of you who  share my conviction that many nations  including these three still need to be  liberated thank you
 

